const faqs = [
  ["What is prior art translation?","Prior art translation provides target-language access to foreign-language patents, technical publications, scientific literature, and other materials selected for patent research. Depending on the purpose, the work may range from rapid document screening and summaries to selected-passage translation, complete professional translation, or independently verified translation."],
  ["Do I need to translate every foreign-language patent found in a prior-art search?","Usually not. Large search result sets can often be screened first using abstracts, AI-assisted translation, bibliographic information, summaries, or targeted linguistic review. Documents that appear more important can then be escalated to selected-passage or complete professional translation. The appropriate workflow depends on the research instructions and should be determined by the patent professionals directing the project."],
  ["Can AI translate prior art for patent research?","AI-assisted translation can be highly useful for document screening, initial comprehension, terminology extraction, large-volume triage, and identifying material for deeper review. Higher-risk or decision-critical content should receive the level of qualified human translation and review appropriate to its intended use. AI should support the research process, not independently determine patentability, infringement, FTO, validity, or the legal significance of a reference."],
  ["Is an abstract enough for patentability research?","An abstract can be extremely useful for initial screening, but it does not contain every detail of the complete patent document. If a reference appears potentially important, researchers may request translation of relevant claims, specification passages, examples, embodiments, figures, or the complete document."],
  ["Should FTO translation focus on patent claims?","Claims are particularly important in FTO analysis because patent rights are defined by claims, and FTO research is jurisdiction- and legal-status-specific. Patent professionals may also request relevant portions of the specification, definitions, figures, prosecution materials, or patent-family documents for additional context. Stepes translates the material identified for review; qualified patent professionals determine claim scope and FTO conclusions."],
  ["What is the difference between prior-art translation for patentability and FTO?","Patentability research generally considers earlier disclosures that may be relevant to whether an invention satisfies applicable patentability requirements. FTO research considers potentially applicable patent rights that may affect a product, process, or commercial activity in a particular jurisdiction. The same patent may be relevant to both questions, but the research purpose and translation priorities can differ significantly."],
  ["Can Stepes translate only selected patent claims or passages?","Yes. Prior-art projects can include individual claims, groups of claims, specific numbered paragraphs, embodiments, examples, tables, figures, or other selected portions. Clearly defined source identifiers help maintain traceability and make the limits of the translated scope clear."],
  ["When should a complete foreign patent be professionally translated?","Full professional translation may be appropriate when a reference becomes sufficiently important that researchers, attorneys, engineers, or experts need complete access to its technical context. Examples can include decision-critical patentability references, important FTO patents, high-priority invalidity material, litigation documents, licensing reviews, and due-diligence matters."],
  ["Does prior-art translation need to be certified?","Not routinely. Screening, internal research, and early-stage analysis generally do not automatically require certified translation. Certification or other formal documentation may become appropriate when requested for a particular court, patent office, administrative proceeding, expert process, or other formal use. The receiving authority and counsel should determine the applicable requirement."],
  ["Can Stepes translate scientific papers and other non-patent literature?","Yes. Prior-art and patent research may involve scientific papers, journals, conference proceedings, standards, technical manuals, theses, product materials, and other foreign-language technical content in addition to patents. Translation scope can range from summaries or selected passages to complete professional translation."],
  ["Does Stepes determine whether a reference qualifies as prior art or invalidates a patent?","No. Stepes provides translation, technical-language, formatting, and multilingual workflow support. We do not render legal opinions regarding prior-art status, patentability, validity, invalidity, infringement, claim construction, freedom to operate, critical dates, or evidentiary significance. Those determinations remain with the client and its qualified patent counsel or other patent professionals."],
].map(([q,a])=>({q,a}));

